From light ![Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.16.20](/uploads/2124d8a1684e572df8b35b4f6dc88106/Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.16.20.png){width=160 height=68} to dark ![Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.16.45](/uploads/c4149d0eae33aee745fd0026e8f9944d/Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.16.45.png){width=160 height=64}

From dark ![Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.17.09](/uploads/403435fbd6394db830d0319b39a65b3b/Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.17.09.png){width=160 height=65} to light ![Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.17.25](/uploads/235231bfa7002cdfc137b857479d8d67/Screenshot_2025-11-20_at_18.17.25.png){width=160 height=66}

There are two ways to force a QWidget to repaint itself. The preferred choice would be to call ```QWidget::update()``` which schedules an asynchronous redraw of the QWidget through the UI eventloop. This will basically also be, what setStyleSheet does internally. To not do it asynchronous, one could also use ```QWidget::repaint()``` to force an immediate redraw, but that could lead to a hanging UI depending on the amount of redrawing that needs to be done.

So in the case above, I would replace the code with
```
update();
```

If it is just the stylesheet that gets changed and ```QWidget::update()``` alone does not the trick, it could be preset by repolishing the stylesheet. That would look something like this

```
style()->unpolish(this);
style()->polish(this);
update();
```

If this also does not work, last resort would be a reassignment of the current stylesheet. Still hacky, but less so than the current code:

```
setStyleSheet(styleSheet());
```

We don't change the stylesheet, this is done because of the switch between Light and Dark mode.

A single `style()->polish(this)` without the `unpolish()` and `update()` seems to work in this case. The two others do not work.

@dogukangulyasar Try using this, and remember to add a comment why this is needed.

I tried all 3 approaches, but unfortunately none of them switches the mode to the desired one:

1. In DataSourceTab::updateStyleSheet(), I tried calling `style()->polish(this)` without `unpolish()` and without `update()`:
```
void DataSourceTab::updateStyleSheet()
{
#ifdef Q_OS_MAC
    style()->polish(this);
#endif
}
```

2. I tried schedule to repaint via `update()`:
```
void DataSourceTab::updateStyleSheet()
{
#ifdef Q_OS_MAC
    update();
#endif
}
```

3. I tried the full sequence: `unpolish()`, `polish()`, and `update()` together:
```
void DataSourceTab::updateStyleSheet()
{
#ifdef Q_OS_MAC
    style()->unpolish(this);
    style()->polish(this);
    update();
#endif
}
```

None of these worked on my side. The only method I could verify as working is the one implemented in the PR.

Do you have another suggestion or did I missed something?

